## 🧩 Shared Optimization Techniques

### 1. Component Memoization Strategy

Apply this strategy to both web and native components:

```tsx
// When to use React.memo:
// 1. For pure functional components
// 2. When props change infrequently
// 3. For expensive rendering operations

// Component with expensive rendering
const DataGrid = React.memo(function DataGrid({ data, columns }) {
  // Implementation...
  return (/* grid rendering */);
}, (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  // Custom comparison for complex props
  return (
    prevProps.data.length === nextProps.data.length &&
    prevProps.data.every((item, i) => item.id === nextProps.data[i].id) &&
    prevProps.columns === nextProps.columns
  );
});

// Memoizing expensive calculations
function ChartContainer({ data }) {
  // Recalculated only when data changes
  const processedData = useMemo(() => {
    return data.map(/* expensive transformations */);
  }, [data]);
  
  return <Chart data={processedData} />;
}

// Stable callback references
function ProductList({ onProductSelect }) {
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {
    onProductSelect(id);
  }, [onProductSelect]);
  
  return (
    <>
      {products.map(product => (
        <Product 
          key={product.id}
          onClick={handleSelect}
          productId={product.id}
        />
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
```

### 2. State Management Optimization

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid: Deeply nested state objects
const [state, setState] = useState({
  user: {
    preferences: {
      theme: 'light',
      notifications: {
        email: true,
        push: false
      }
    }
  }
});

// Later updating deeply nested property:
setState(prev => ({
  ...prev,
  user: {
    ...prev.user,
    preferences: {
      ...prev.user.preferences,
      notifications: {
        ...prev.user.preferences.notifications,
        push: true
      }
    }
  }
}));

// ✅ Better: Flatten state when possible
const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
const [emailNotifications, setEmailNotifications] = useState(true);
const [pushNotifications, setPushNotifications] = useState(false);

// Clean, targeted updates:
setPushNotifications(true);

// ✅ Alternative: Use a reducer for complex state
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(userReducer, initialState);

// Later:
dispatch({ type: 'TOGGLE_PUSH_NOTIFICATIONS' });
```

### 3. Virtualization for Long Lists

For both platforms, virtualize lists that may contain many items:

```tsx
// Web implementation with react-window
import { FixedSizeList } from 'react-window';

function VirtualizedList({ items }) {
  const Row = ({ index, style }) => (
    <div style={style}>
      <div>{items[index].name}</div>
    </div>
  );

  return (
    <FixedSizeList
      height={400}
      width="100%"
      itemCount={items.length}
      itemSize={35}
    >
      {Row}
    </FixedSizeList>
  );
}

// Native implementation
import { FlatList } from 'react-native';

function VirtualizedList({ items }) {
  const renderItem = ({ item }) => (
    <View style={styles.item}>
      <Text>{item.name}</Text>
    </View>
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={items}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      keyExtractor={item => item.id}
      initialNumToRender={10}
      maxToRenderPerBatch={10}
      windowSize={5}
    />
  );
}
```

